//
// A generic function declares one type variable: a header with a second is refused once,
// where the second is written, and the template never instantiates.
//
// The header used to stop at the comma and report five syntax errors (`Expect token >`,
// `(`, `{`, `;`, `}`); a second variable's bounds went unread, so its use in the body added a
// sixth.
// @falsified-at: 04f5791cf — exit 1 -> 0, expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1
//   CHANNEL: expectations (the refusal is what moves; the program is refused on both trees).
//   ARMED: nothing.
//   WITNESS: on the control the header reports five syntax errors, from `Expect token >`.
//   HOLDS: the program is refused on both trees; only the messages move.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `pair_up` declares 2 type variables (`K`, `V`), and a generic function declares one — `<K>`

fn pair_up<K: Printable, V: Printable>(k: K, v: V) -> text { "{k}:{v}" }

fn main() {
  println("{pair_up(1, "a")}");
}
